/etc/init from the Micronix 1.6 standalone

There is no source for init, so this is the disassembly, and the names in
init.ctl are not guesses: they come from watching it run.  The simulator
prints the address of every system call a program makes, so one boot of
the standalone names every library stub in the program by what it did -
_stat is at 43f3 because that is where the stat calls came from.

    disas -f init.ctl init

It is Whitesmith's C: c.ent at 3f87 and c.ret at 3f8e, arguments pushed
right to left, results in BC.  The syscall stubs are in the data segment,
which starts at 3fe2, not in the text - which is why the addresses in a
trace of it are above the text size.

What it does, in the order the trace shows: closes every inherited
descriptor, sets all fifteen signals, opens /dev/root and /dev/swap,
creates /etc/utmp and /etc/mtab, appends to /usr/adm/wtmp, forks, and
then opens /etc/ttys with fopen and reads it a line at a time with fgets,
stat'ing each terminal named in it.  Only ttyA has a login entry.

On 1.6 in the simulator it does not get past that enumeration - see
hwsim/DISKS.  1.67 does, and reaches a shell.
